// Info Wave W5 page 7/9 — "maytag refrigerator not cooling" (350/mo, TP3000 KD0).
// Maytag = Whirlpool platform. Differentiator = 10-year limited parts warranty
// (check your dates) + shared Whirlpool cooling parts. Cross-links whirlpool
// anchor + siblings. Food-safety block (USDA/FDA). Safe DIY only. $89.

import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ServiceHero from '../../../components/ServiceHero.astro';
import { MAIN_PHONE } from '../../../data/branches';
import { BRANCH_LOCATIONS_GEO_NEUTRAL } from '../../../lib/build-location-array';

const phone = MAIN_PHONE;
const phoneRaw = MAIN_PHONE.replace(/\D/g, '');
const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/refrigerator-repair/maytag-not-cooling/";

const title = "Maytag Refrigerator Not Cooling? Causes & Fix";
const description = "Maytag refrigerator not cooling? It's built on the Whirlpool platform — dirty coils, the start relay, the evaporator fan, or defrost. Check your 10-year limited parts warranty first. Safe checks, food-safety timing, when to call.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Maytag refrigerator not cooling?",
    a: "Maytag refrigerators are built on the Whirlpool platform, so the usual causes are the same: dirty condenser coils, a failed compressor start relay (often 'clicking' at the back), a failed evaporator fan ('freezer cold, fridge warm'), a defrost system iced over the evaporator, the control board, and — the serious end — a locked compressor or sealed-system fault. Start by cleaning the coils and checking airflow. Before you pay for a compressor or sealed-system repair, check your Maytag warranty — many Maytag refrigerators carry a 10-year limited parts warranty that can cover the compressor. The coils and airflow are yours to check; the relay, board, and sealed system are a tech job."
  },
  {
    q: "Does the Maytag 10-year warranty cover a refrigerator that won't cool?",
    a: "It can — many Maytag refrigerators carry a 10-year limited parts warranty, and on a lot of models that covers the compressor specifically. It's a parts warranty (it typically covers the part, not the labor to install it), and the exact terms depend on your model and purchase date, so the first move is to find your original purchase paperwork and your model and serial number, then check the warranty terms for your unit. If a locked compressor or sealed-system fault is the diagnosis, that warranty can turn a big-ticket repair into a much smaller one. We're glad to diagnose it so you know exactly what you're claiming."
  },
  {
    q: "My Maytag freezer is cold but the fridge is warm — what's wrong?",
    a: "That split — cold freezer, warm fridge — is the classic evaporator fan failure, same as on any Whirlpool-platform fridge. The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge; when it fails or frost from a defrost fault jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. On Maytag the frost usually traces back to a defrost heater, sensor, or control that's stopped running its cycle. Both are fixable, tech-level repairs inside the cabinet. Keep the doors closed while you wait — the fridge food is on roughly a four-hour clock."
  },
  {
    q: "What's the clicking sound from the back of my Maytag?",
    a: "A click every few minutes from the bottom-rear, with the fridge slowly warming, is often the compressor start relay cycling — the relay tries to start the compressor, fails, trips its overload, and tries again. On the Whirlpool platform Maytag uses, that relay is a common and well-understood no-cool part. Don't keep running it in that state; the repeated starting stresses the compressor. This is a tech diagnosis and repair — and if the compressor itself turns out to be the fault, that's exactly where your 10-year parts warranty may come in."
  },
  {
    q: "How long is food safe in a Maytag that stopped cooling?",
    a: "Per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door kept closed, and a full freezer holds a safe temperature for roughly 48 hours (24 hours if half-full) if you keep it shut. Perishable food — meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, leftovers — held above 40°F for 2 hours or more should be thrown out. When in doubt, throw it out, and never taste food to check. That 4-hour fridge window is why a no-cool refrigerator is a same-day call — keep the doors closed and get a tech out before it closes."
  }
];

const schemaJsons = [
