---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<Fragment slot="head-scripts">
  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/maytag-not-cooling"
  eyebrow="Maytag Refrigerator Not Cooling · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Maytag Refrigerator Not Cooling? Check the Warranty First"
  subtitle="Maytag runs on the Whirlpool platform — coils, start relay, evaporator fan, or defrost. But before you pay for a compressor, check your 10-year limited parts warranty. Here's what to look at and how long your food stays safe."
  heroImageTitle="Maytag Refrigerator Not Cooling Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A no-cool Maytag is Whirlpool-platform parts — and maybe a warranty claim.</h2>

    <p><strong>Maytag refrigerators are built on the Whirlpool platform, so a running-but-warm Maytag comes down to the same short list: dirty condenser coils, a failed compressor start relay (often "clicking" at the back), a failed evaporator fan ("freezer cold, fridge warm"), a defrost system iced over, the control board, and the serious end — a locked compressor or sealed-system fault. Clean the coils and check airflow first. Then, before you pay for anything major, check your warranty: many Maytag refrigerators carry a 10-year limited parts warranty that can cover the compressor. It's a parts warranty tied to your model and purchase date, so find your paperwork. The coils and airflow are yours to check; the relay, board, and sealed system are a tech job. A no-cool fridge is a same-day call — your food is on a clock. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Food is on a clock:</strong> per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door closed. Keep the doors shut, move critical items to a cooler with ice if you can, and get a tech out before that window closes. Full food-safety details are in the section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 WARRANTY DIFFERENTIATOR -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Check the 10-year limited parts warranty</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">On a Maytag, the big-ticket repair may already be covered.</h2>

    <p>Maytag's headline is durability, and many of its refrigerators back that with a 10-year limited parts warranty — and on a lot of models that covers the compressor, which is exactly the part that fails at the expensive end of a no-cool call. That makes the warranty check worth doing <em>before</em> you approve any sealed-system work.</p>

    <p>Two honest caveats. First, it's a <strong>parts</strong> warranty — it typically covers the part, not the labor to install it, so there's usually still a repair cost. Second, the exact coverage depends on your model and your original purchase date, so pull your purchase paperwork and note the model and serial number off the sticker inside the fridge, then check the terms for your specific unit. We'll diagnose the fault so you know precisely what you're claiming — and if it's the compressor, that warranty can turn a big repair into a small one.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Maytag stops cooling</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dirty condenser coils (check first)</h3>
      <p>Coils caked in dust and pet hair can't shed the compressor's heat, so the fridge slowly loses cooling. The most common homeowner-fixable cause, and worse in an LA summer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Compressor start relay — the "clicking"</h3>
      <p>A click every few minutes from the bottom-rear with the fridge warming is often the start relay failing to start the compressor. A common, well-understood Whirlpool-platform no-cool part — but a tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan — "freezer cold, fridge warm"</h3>
      <p>The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge. When it fails or frost jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. A tech job inside the cabinet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Defrost system iced over</h3>
      <p>A failed defrost heater, sensor, or control lets frost build on the evaporator until air can't pass and cooling drops. The tell is a wall of frost behind the freezer's back panel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Compressor / sealed system</h3>
      <p>If the compressor runs but doesn't cool or won't start, the sealed system is the fault — the higher-ticket end, and the part your 10-year warranty may cover. A gauges-and-tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Vacuum the condenser coils</strong> behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate. This alone fixes a lot of gradual no-cool cases.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the interior vents</strong> so food isn't blocking airflow between compartments.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door seal and temperature setting,</strong> and confirm the setting wasn't bumped warm.</li>
      <li><strong>Give it room and a power-cycle</strong> — pull the fridge off the wall, unplug a minute, restart.</li>
      <li><strong>Find your model, serial, and purchase date</strong> — you'll want them to check the 10-year parts warranty if a compressor turns out to be the fault.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> coils, vents, seal, temperature, and a power-cycle are safe. Do not open the sealed refrigeration system, test the compressor or start relay, or replace the control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FOOD SAFETY -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Heat-wave food safety — when to call today</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A warm fridge is a clock. Here are the numbers.</h2>

    <p>A refrigerator that's stopped cooling is a race against the clock, and in an LA heat wave that clock runs faster. Per USDA and FDA guidance:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Refrigerator: about 4 hours</strong> of safe food with the door kept closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Freezer: roughly 48 hours full, 24 hours half-full,</strong> if the door stays shut.</li>
      <li><strong>Above 40°F for 2 hours or more → discard it.</strong> That covers meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, and leftovers.</li>
      <li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out</strong> — and never taste food to decide. Frozen food can be safely refrozen only if it still has ice crystals or is at 40°F or below.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>That's why a no-cool Maytag is a same-day call. Keep the doors closed, move the critical items to a cooler with ice, and get a tech out before the four-hour fridge window closes. We answer phones 24/7 and run same-day across most of the area. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Coils clean, still warm? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the coils are clean, the vents are clear, and the Maytag still won't cool, the fault is inside — the start relay, the evaporator fan, the defrost system, or the compressor and sealed system. Because Maytag shares the Whirlpool platform, our techs carry the common start relays, fan motors, and defrost parts for a one-visit fix where possible — and we'll tell you straight if the compressor is a warranty claim.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a> guide, the shared-platform <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/whirlpool-not-cooling/">Whirlpool not cooling</a> page, and the sibling brands <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/kitchenaid-not-cooling/">KitchenAid</a> and <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/amana-not-cooling/">Amana</a>. If it's the fridge warm while the freezer's fine, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/refrigerator-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Maytag refrigerator not cooling: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Maytag refrigerator not cooling? Call today — food is on a clock.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

